+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql against the production snapshot
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the production snapshot while
+`users.email` is required (`NOT NULL`) and case-insensitively unique, because
+the snapshot violates both conditions:
+
+| Constraint | Offending rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NOT NULL` | `id = 17` (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL` |
+| unique `lower(email)` | ids `1`, `4`, `23` are all `ana.silva@example.com` / `Ana.Silva@example.com` |
+
+Verified against `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`:
+
+```
+nulls: 1
+ci-dups: ana.silva@example.com 3
+```
+
+## Why it is blocked
+
+Making the column `NOT NULL` and adding a case-insensitive unique index
+requires every existing row to satisfy those constraints. The only ways to
+achieve that are to:
+
+- delete or merge duplicate accounts (ids 1, 4, 23), and
+- backfill a real email for id 17 (or delete that row).
+
+Both change customer rows. Support owns that data, and this task explicitly
+forbids the migration from modifying or deleting customer rows. There is no
+SQLite schema change that enforces the constraints over existing rows while
+grandfathering rows that violate them, so the requirement is impossible
+without a data decision that only Support can make.
+
+I have deliberately not worked around this (no partial index that exempts
+NULLs, no trigger-only enforcement for future inserts, no silent data
+rewrite). Each of those would report success while leaving existing customer
+records inconsistent with the intended schema.
+
+## State of the work
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` implements the intended schema change for
+  databases whose data already satisfies it (fresh databases): `email TEXT
+  NOT NULL` plus a unique index on `lower(email)`.
+- The migration begins with a guard that aborts with
+  `CHECK constraint failed: existing_users_have_null_or_duplicate_emails`
+  before any DDL if NULL or duplicate emails exist. It therefore cannot leave
+  the production database partially rebuilt, but it also cannot be applied to
+  the snapshot.
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails by design: it requires the migration
+  to apply to the snapshot, which is exactly what is blocked.
+
+## What Support needs to decide
+
+1. Correct, or remove, the `email = NULL` account (id 17).
+2. Resolve the duplicate accounts ids 1, 4, and 23 — decide which record is
+   canonical and what to do with the others (merge, reassign, or delete).
+
+Once those rows are resolved, `002_unique_email.sql` can be applied unchanged.
